;;; `std.elim/no-room-left` — every value excluded for some `a`: nothing left
;;; to force, so ⊥.
;;;
;;; ── The rule the corpus loaded and never satisfied ─────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One of the five rules the census found *compiled, activated and waiting*
;;; with the state that fires them never arising (§3b). Every one of the five
;;; asserts `(false)`, and the finding was not that a refutation rule fails to
;;; fire in a corpus of solvable puzzles — that is expected — but that it had
;;; never fired in a corpus of **broken** ones either: `examples/broken/` is
;;; thirty-seven parse and load errors and not one program that loads, runs,
;;; and is inconsistent.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to it ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `01_domain_elimination.ein` with one more exclusion. There the rows read
;;;
;;;   S1  V1 ✗  V2 ✗  V3 ·     one candidate left  ⟹ forced
;;;
;;; and here
;;;
;;;   S1  V1 ✗  V2 ✗  V3 ✗     no candidate left   ⟹ ⊥
;;;
;;; so the two files differ by exactly the fact the rule is about, and between
;;; them they cover the `forall`'s two ends: the last survivor, and none.
;;;
;;; ── Why the ⊥ is this rule's, and what that cost ──────────────────────────
;;;
;;; **Only `no-room-left` is activated here.** `01_domain_elimination.ein`
;;; declares both activators and this file declares one, which is a deliberate
;;; asymmetry: on a fully excluded row *both* rules match, because "every value
;;; but `?v` is excluded" holds for every `?v` once every value is excluded —
;;; so `domain-elimination` would force `(holds S1 V1)` against the stored
;;; negative and reach `(false)` by a different road. The verdict would be the
;;; same and the diagnosis would not.
;;;
;;; Leaving it out is what makes this a test of `no-room-left` rather than of
;;; the module, and it was measured rather than assumed: with both activated, a
;;; `no-room-left` mutated to quantify over the wrong type stops firing and the
;;; whole suite still passes, because elimination refutes anyway. With one, the
;;; same mutant turns this file into a `Solution` and is caught. An expectation
;;; is made of facts and cannot say which rule produced them — the `route`
;;; residue Q-M1c.2 parks (plans/m1c_external_validation/open_questions.md) —
;;; so where two rules can reach one verdict, a fixture has to separate them by
;;; **activation**.
;;;
;;; The priorities are still why the module can afford to ship both:
;;; `no-room-left` is 110 and elimination is 400, so in a program that
;;; activates the pair the exhausted row is diagnosed before the forced
;;; collision is.

(import std.macro :symbols (forall))
(import std.elim  :symbols (no-room-left))

(relation is-a  T T        :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation holds Slot Value :why "{?1} holds {?2}")

(is-a S1 Slot) (is-a S2 Slot) (is-a S3 Slot)
(is-a V1 Value) (is-a V2 Value) (is-a V3 Value)

(functional holds 0 1)
(total      holds 0)
(no-room-left holds is-a Slot Value)   ; and *not* domain-elimination — see above

(not (holds S1 V1))
(not (holds S1 V2))
(not (holds S1 V3))        ; ⊥ — S1 has nowhere left to go

(query
  :goal (holds ?slot ?value)
  :no-hypothesis (is-a holds)
  :expect (false))
